{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "299",
    "case_summary": "A man in his 30s presented with a mass in the right nostril that had been growing for about 1 year. The lesion bled with minimal trauma but did not obstruct his breathing. The patient did not smoke and denied any medical history of chronic rhinosinusitis.\n\nPhysical examination revealed a 1-cm, nontender, exophytic, verrucous, skin-colored plaque on the right nasal vestibule.\n\n- Histologic examination:  Image Title: Histologic sections (Figure C)  Image Description: Papillary fronds with slender fibrovascular cores that are lined by nonkeratinizing squamous epithelium (original magnification ×200)    Image Title: Higher magnification histology (Figure D)  Image Description: Shows mucin-containing goblet cells. Neither cytologic atypia nor atypical mitotic figures are present (original magnification ×400)    Clinical Images:  Image Title: Clinical presentation (Figure A and B)  Image Description: An exophytic, verrucous, and skin-colored plaque on the right nasal vestibule",
